"""Visible experiments, not cosmetic rarity skins.
Each family explores a different practical change in each episode. Trace-making
finds have their own directions so bindings are never added to a painted mark.
"""
TRACE_FAMILIES={168,169,175,179,183,187,189,191,209,212,220,239,240,249,253,259,263,269,274,275,279,282}
# label | object construction | trace / observation construction | study question
ROWS='''
A first attempt|Keep the original subject exactly.|Keep the original subject exactly.|
A mended attempt|Show ONE localized crude repair chosen to fit the actual material and use: frayed cord is spliced with loose fiber; torn hide is patched or pinned; a cracked hollow is plugged with clay; broken wood can be splinted; a chipped edge can be reworked. Choose only the physically appropriate repair and show its useful contact clearly. NEVER add a universal wooden splint to every material.|Show a damaged or interrupted trace resumed on the remaining natural surface, with an obvious discontinuity where the maker worked around the break.|A damaged attempt can still carry a useful idea when someone finds a way to continue it.
A substitute found nearby|Replace a secondary support with an unworked naturally forked twig or curved bark scrap that performs the same job awkwardly; make the substitution prominent.|Use the same crude mark or observation on a naturally curved bark surface, with rough fibers affecting the result; no prepared writing board.|The same idea can survive a change of supporting material.
A smaller working contact|Reduce only the active contact area markedly while leaving the rough support large; show the small working part and its local effect clearly.|Make the meaningful trace with only a fingertip-sized contact instead of a broad gesture; show a larger incomplete trial beside it on the same surface.|Concentrating an action changes the detail that can be controlled.
Two contacts instead of one|Show two uneven working contacts held side by side by one simple rough root tie; no regular mechanism or finished frame.|Show two differently sized rough traces produced at once by paired natural contact points; a forked twig nearby suggests how they were made.|Combining contacts lets one action leave more than one effect.
A broad contact|Widen the active contact using an overlapping natural fragment, with one crude loose attachment visible; keep the object unfinished.|A broad palm or soft pad has spread the same underlying trace into a blunt wide version; narrow earlier trials remain visible nearby.|Spreading contact changes how force or material reaches a surface.
A shared attempt|Show two mismatched rough grips or supports at opposite ends of the same small experimental assembly; no people needed.|Unequal coarse traces overlap on one natural surface, suggesting two different hands trying the same idea.|Several people can compare their actions through what remains of a shared attempt.
Packed for movement|Show the experiment partly nested in a ragged fold of bark or hide with a simple loose tie, the active part exposed and protected rather than hidden.|A portable fragment bearing the trace is partly wrapped in rough hide, with its meaningful surface exposed; no frame or polished pendant.|Protecting an example makes an idea easier to carry elsewhere.
A useful natural fork|The active part is wedged into the crotch of an unworked forked branch; show the fork doing the holding with minimal loose fiber.|The trace uses a natural branching crack as part of its shape or grouping, with uneven pigment or impressions continuing around it.|An existing shape can do part of the work before anything is deliberately made.
Braced at the weak point|Add one short rough brace only where the object is bending or splitting; the rest remains unsupported and crude.|Show a trace interrupted by a ridge and resumed on a small adjacent surface, using the natural division to organize it.|Recognizing a weak or obstructed point suggests where a small change matters most.
Less material in the way|Show part of a bulky support crudely scooped or split away to expose the working area, with removed rough fragments nearby.|The maker has crudely rubbed away part of a dirty surface before leaving the trace; uneven cleared and uncleared areas are both visible.|Removing material can reveal a useful surface or make an action easier to control.
The unfinished comparison|One incomplete working part remains beside a slightly more usable but still clumsy version, on the same patch of ground; a single archaeological grouping, no panels.|One hesitant partial trace lies beside a more complete version on the same unprepared surface; neither is refined or regular.|Incomplete attempts preserve the steps by which an idea becomes usable.
The failed trial|Show a clear failure at the working contact: slipped binding, collapsed edge or broken support appropriate to this subject. Keep the failed pieces close enough to understand their intended connection.|Show a visibly smeared, misaligned or collapsed version of the trace beside one small clearer surviving portion; no lettering.|A failure can reveal which part of an idea needs to change.
An old piece used again|Make a worn discarded fragment the new support for the working part, with its former use scar visibly off to one side; no refined recycling equipment.|A new crude trace crosses an older faded unrelated trace on the same natural surface, both still distinguishable without writing.|Existing objects can acquire a second use when their remaining properties are noticed.
Both sides tried|Show the rough assembly tipped so two different working faces are visible, one used heavily and the other only tentatively tested.|Show one irregular folded or fractured surface with different rough trial traces on its two visible faces; no book or hinged tablet.|Trying another face can reveal possibilities hidden by a familiar way of holding an object.
A tiny trial|Show a very small clumsy specimen beside a few wild seeds for scale; emphasize its reduced working contact and disproportionately coarse attachment.|A tiny recognizable trace is crowded into a natural small hollow, with a larger incomplete mark outside it for comparison.|A small trial tests whether an idea still works when space and material are scarce.
A heavier demand|Show a larger awkward working piece straining a short crude support; compressed contact and a sagging root tie make the added load visible.|A much larger rough trace crosses a natural ridge and becomes uneven where the maker could not maintain the same contact.|Changing scale exposes limits that a smaller attempt can hide.
An off-center solution|Move the working contact visibly to one side of the grip or support to reach around an obstruction, using crooked natural parts.|The meaningful trace is deliberately offset around a protruding stone or knot in bark, with the obstruction clearly visible.|An uneven arrangement can solve a problem that a centered arrangement cannot.
A protective lining|Place a thin torn hide or bark lining only between the working part and its rough support, with an exposed folded edge showing the added layer.|A thin uneven clay smear receives the trace on a coarse natural surface, with raw rock or bark visible around the edges; no prepared plaque.|A thin intervening layer changes how two surfaces interact.
The repeated action|Show the active part beside a short irregular row of three distinct contact results such as small dents, scrapings or impressions appropriate to its function; no numbers.|Show three imperfect attempts at the same underlying trace, with changing spacing and depth, never a formal script or decorative border.|Repeating an action makes differences between attempts easier to notice.
A steadier base|Prop the crude assembly on two unequal natural stones and a short splinter so its active contact stays clear; no built stand or finished frame.|A marked fragment is steadied between unequal natural stones, its rough working surface held at a useful angle.|A stable support can make a difficult action more repeatable.
Taken apart to understand|Show the crude working part, support and loose binding immediately adjacent as a coherent small dismantled find, with matching contact scars; no exploded diagram or labels.|A rough impression and the natural object that made it lie just apart with their matching contours obvious.|Separating parts reveals which surface or connection produces the useful effect.
A softer hold|Add a ragged moss or hide pad around the hand-contact area only; the harder active surface stays exposed.|The trace is made by a soft moss pad beside a harsher incomplete twig-made trial; the natural applicators lie nearby.|A softer contact changes both comfort and the character of a result.
After a little heat|Show one working edge or joint locally darkened by a small heat exposure, with an untreated loose fragment for comparison; no kiln, metallurgy or finished pottery.|Two crude samples of the trace material lie together, one partly scorched and distorted and one untreated; no firing installation.|Small exposures invite comparison of how materials change under heat.
After wetting|Show damp darkening localized to the active joint or contact, a few droplets and a shifted or swollen connection; no plumbing device.|A wet rough trace spreads differently from a dry trial beside it on the same irregular surface.|Moisture changes how materials hold, move and leave traces.
A little grit added|Show coarse sand trapped at the active contact while the surrounding support stays bare, with tiny rubbed particles nearby.|The same crude trace appears on a gritty patch and a smoother natural patch, showing visibly different broken edges.|Small grains between surfaces can change grip, abrasion and the marks left behind.
Pulled into place|Show a short frayed root loop pulling the working part against its support, visible tension and an awkward bend; no bow mechanism unless already part of the subject.|The trace follows the edge of a roughly tensioned plant cord lying across the natural surface, without measured divisions.|A flexible line can guide or hold an action when pulled taut.
Easy to loosen|Show one short rough toggle twig holding a loose binding at the support, partly withdrawn so the connection can open; no engineered latch.|A removable natural pebble or leaf masks part of the trace-making surface and lies partly lifted to reveal the uncovered result.|A temporary constraint can be useful because it can be removed.
Two materials compared|Place a second rough working contact of a different naturally available material beside the original, each with a small local use scar; no standardized test kit.|Two crude versions of the same trace use different natural materials or pigments on the same raw surface, with small source fragments nearby.|Comparing materials separates an idea from the first substance used to try it.
Inside the working hollow|Use a close oblique view into the assembly's natural gap or hollow so hidden contact surfaces and trapped residue are visible; do not invent a cavity in solid objects, use the gap beneath or between their contacts.|Show a trace tucked beneath a natural overhang or inside a broken hollow, with the restricted access and uneven result visible.|Looking into a sheltered contact can reveal an effect hidden from ordinary view.
Three stages left together|Show three connected stages of the same rough construction: unworked raw fragment, abandoned partial piece and clumsy useful specimen. One coherent scattered find, no panels or labels.|Show an unmarked patch, a partial attempt and a recognizable rough trace across one irregular surface; no ruled sequence.|Keeping stages together allows another observer to reconstruct a process.
The idea carried across|Show the crude working principle tried with a markedly different natural support while preserving its active contact: bark instead of a bulky wood base, or a forked branch instead of a stone prop.|Show the same crude motif or observation transferred from a natural rock face onto a small irregular clay or bark fragment beside it; no writing or seal.|A recognizable principle can be tried in a new setting without copying every detail.
'''
EPISODES=[tuple(line.split('|')) for line in ROWS.strip().splitlines()]
assert len(EPISODES)==32
assert all(len(e)==4 for e in EPISODES)
